            <ArticleTitle>Contrast media and pain in peripheral arteriography.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>S335-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Pain caused by x-ray contrast media in peripheral arteriography was assessed by behavioral changes of nonanesthetized, unrestrained rats. All ionic monomeric contrast media caused severe pain in a concentration of 300 mg I/ml. Dilution of contrast media markedly reduced pain. Sodium salt solutions were considerably more painful than meglumine salt solutions. The intravenous cholegraphic agent iodipamide did not cause vascular pain in a concentration of 300 mg I/ml, although its systemic toxicity is high. Pain in arteriography was attributable primarily to the high osmotic pressure of contrast media solutions rather than to their chemotoxicity.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
